---
/**
 * The qualifying text for `VerifiedBadge` (src/components/VerifiedBadge.tsx),
 * rendered as real, always-visible page content — never a tooltip, never
 * `.visually-hidden` — so it is reachable on touch devices and present in the
 * accessible tree for a screen-reader user who lands directly on the badge
 * (every `VerifiedBadge` on the same page should pass this component's id as
 * its `descriptionId` prop, wiring `aria-describedby` to this paragraph).
 *
 * Every page that renders a `VerifiedBadge` MUST also render exactly one
 * `SignatureNote`. It states plainly what the badge does and does NOT mean:
 *
 *   - what ran, at the INDEX level (S2 PR-2, real): the build runs the
 *     conduit CLI's own verifier (cmd/registry-verify, --require-root, same
 *     compiled-in trust anchors) against the index's ROOT signature, rollback
 *     floor, and 7-day staleness window, and refuses to build from anything
 *     that fails. The index every page here renders is therefore
 *     cryptographically authenticated — an index the conduit CLI would refuse
 *     cannot reach this site.
 *   - what ran, at the VERSION level (WS4 S3, real): the same build also ran
 *     the verifier's --artifacts pass — it fetched every referenced signature
 *     and SLSA provenance BUNDLE (never the binaries; bounded at the CLI's
 *     1 MiB cap), verified them against the trust anchors and the connector's
 *     pinned identity, and checked that the provenance subject digest binds
 *     to the sha256 the index declares. The badge renders that verdict:
 *     pass / fail(reason) / not_attempted(reason).
 *   - what did NOT run: the site never downloads the artifact binaries
 *     (connector or processor — WS4 S5 covers processors with the same
 *     verdicts), so the badges vouch for the bytes the INDEX declares, not
 *     the bytes a download serves you today. Real verification of the actual
 *     file you install happens at install time, in the CLI, against the same
 *     anchors.
 *
 * The /verify page (src/pages/verify.astro) is the full, human-readable
 * record: what was verified, how, when (as-of dates), and what the verdicts
 * do not mean.
 */
export const SIGNATURE_NOTE_ID = 'signature-note';
---

<p id={SIGNATURE_NOTE_ID}>
  Badges on this page are build-time verification verdicts: the site&rsquo;s build cryptographically
  verifies the signed index itself (root signature, rollback floor, 7-day staleness window) and then
  verifies every version&rsquo;s signature and SLSA provenance bundles against the trust anchors,
  the publisher&rsquo;s pinned identity, and the index-declared sha256 &mdash; pass, fail, or not
  attempted, each with a reason. What this site still does not do: it never downloads the artifact
  binaries (connector or processor), so the badges vouch for the bytes the index declares, not the
  bytes a download serves you today. That check happens when you run
  <code>conduit connectors install &lt;name&gt;</code> (or <code>conduit processors install</code>),
  which verifies the actual bytes against the same anchors before installing anything. Full details:
  the
  <a href="/verify/">verification report</a>.
</p>
